Thank you everyone for responding so quickly. I'm not sure we are all on the same page if this is a Triton or ptxas issue, or both.

For me, it's a Triton bug. The ptxas bug is only relevant for register ping-pong, which is not what Triton emits.

Currently, Triton emits something like this:

```
load %d0;
load %d1;

Loop:
    mov %a0, %d0; 
    mov %a1, %d1; 
    wgmma.fence;
    wgmma.mma_async {%c}, {%a0}, %b;
    wgmma.commit_group;
    wgmma.mma_async {%c}, {%a1}, %b;
    wgmma.commit_group;
    load %d0;
    load %d1;
    wgmma.wait_group 1;

    bra Loop;
```

The `wait_group 1` is a bug in Triton, because of the RAW hazard:
`mov %a1, %d1` might be executed before `mma_async {%c}, {%a1}, %b`.
With the above loop structure, it needs to say `wait_group 0`.
The code above does not suffer from any ptxas liveness analysis bug.

Pipelining the `mma_async` with input registers requires unrolling the loop to ping-pong between two sets of registers:

```
load %d0;
load %d1;
load %d2;
load %d3;

Loop:
    mov %a0, %d0; 
    mov %a1, %d1; 
    wgmma.fence;
    wgmma.mma_async {%c}, {%a0}, %b;
    wgmma.mma_async {%c}, {%a1}, %b;
    wgmma.commit_group;
    load %d0;
    load %d1;
    wgmma.wait_group 1;

    mov %a2, %d2;
    mov %a3, %d3;
    wgmma.fence;
    wgmma.mma_async {%c}, {%a2}, %b;
    wgmma.mma_async {%c}, {%a3}, %b;
    wgmma.commit_group;
    load %d2;
    load %d3;
    wgmma.wait_group 1;

    bra Loop;
```

`wait_group 1` is sufficient here, *if* ptxas correctly analyzes that `%a0` and `%a1` are live all the way until the second `wait_group 1`. This is not currently the case but will be fixed with ptxas 13.2.

**Disclaimer:** Please don't assume above analysis is correct. My knowledge is limited, and I don't have access to the slack conversation.

Oh, I see. So you intend to emit something like this?

Adjusted from the first example [above](https://github.com/triton-lang/triton/issues/9433#issuecomment-3890904677):

```
load %d0;
load %d1;

Loop:
    mov %a0, %d0; 
    wgmma.fence;
    wgmma.mma_async {%c}, {%a0}, %b;
    wgmma.commit_group;
    load %d0;            // moved up, just for symmetry
    wgmma.wait_group 1;  // new
    mov %a1, %d1;        // moved down
    wgmma.fence;         // new
    wgmma.mma_async {%c}, {%a1}, %b;
    wgmma.commit_group;
    load %d1;
    wgmma.wait_group 1;

    bra Loop;
```

Yeah, I see that this should work if ptxas correctly analyzed that
- `%a0` is live past `load %d0` until after the second `wait_group`
- `%a1` is live past `load %d1` until after the first `wait_group` of the next iteration.

It prevents eliding the copy because the live range of `%a0` overlaps with `%d0`, and `%a1` overlaps with `%d1`.

